1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:10,400
I built the first prototype on a weekend, put it on my own phone, I used it myself two weeks and my screen time was reduced by fifty percent after that and I was like, okay, this is really effective.

2
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:22,000
Actually, when I installed the app on my own phone, twenty seconds afterwards I accidentally ended up opening Twitter and I ran into my own app and I was like, really okay, I'm on to something here.

3
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:23,200
Welcome to Launched.

4
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,800
I'm your host, Charlie Chapman.

5
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:47,120
And today I'm excited to bring you the founder of OneSeq, the app to help you cut down your screen time, Frederick, welcome to the show.

6
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:49,280
Hello, happy to be back.

7
00:00:50,480 --> 00:01:00,960
Yes, and we're saying back because as I actually talked about this in the previous episode, but we did a live episode together in Finland at Arctic Commons.

8
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:01,120
Yes.

9
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,640
And it was a lot of fun.

10
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:04,160
Yes, it was a lot of fun.

11
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,240
Somehow I ended up playing the cymbals on stage with the band and you.

12
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,240
I think we had a hat of percussion instruments handed to us.

13
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:20,560
It was a very interesting vibe, but as is kind of becoming a common theme here, I'm failing at the live recording thing.

14
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,400
And so now we get to do this in a more like kind of proper, longer launched setting anyway.

15
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,760
So it's probably a better episode.

16
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:33,360
But yeah, we get to talk about One Sec in a more traditional launched mode here.

17
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:34,720
So I'm very excited.

18
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:39,200
The story here is really good, and there's a bunch of threads I didn't get to pull on while we were on stage.

19
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:42,160
And so I think at the end of the day, this is going to be fun.

20
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,640
Yeah, I'm really happy we do this.

21
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:49,680
So before we get into One Second and your story and everything.

22
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,200
Let's give people a quick pitch for what One Second is.

23
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,720
Just kind of your elevator pitch so that we have a baseline for what we're building up to.

24
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:05,920
So my elevator pitch is actually always showing the app because it's kind of like a hard thing to explain, but it's super easy to show, right?

25
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,480
So imagine this is your home screen and you have your social media apps there.

26
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:16,560
And whenever you open a social media app like Instagram, instead my app, One Sec, comes into foreground.

27
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,640
Now I have to put it in the area where it's not blurred.

28
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:28,640
And yes, it just intercepts you from opening social media apps to help you be more mindful about when you open these apps.

29
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:44,160
This actually comes from a problem I had myself in 2020 in the first lockdown in Germany where I was just feeling like I was using Instagram way too much, much more than I thought was healthy for me and I didn't feel good about it.

30
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:51,600
But at the same time, I also didn't want to delete it completely because I felt like there is still some value of using social media.

31
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:55,040
And as of today, I still feel the same way.

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,480
But I was just feeling like I have to get back in control.

33
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:00,560
I don't want to be the product anymore.

34
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:06,240
I don't want to use social media like people in Silicon Valley want me to use it.

35
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:09,920
I want to use social media as a tool, how I want to use it.

36
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:18,960
And my problem was really that I had this goal in mind of using it less, but I failed to accomplish it.

37
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:25,200
I was using like all the tools that existed back then, like screen time tools, whatever, and nothing really helped me.

38
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:32,000
And then I was like, yeah, my problem is actually that in the moment when I open the app, my brain doesn't think about what's going on.

39
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:40,560
It's just like a reflex, muscle memory, like autopilot kind of thing, you just open the app because you're stressed or you're bored or whatever.

40
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:51,280
And I was just feeling I need some kind of way that interrupts me when I open an app like Instagram or Twitter or whatever.

41
00:03:51,920 --> 00:04:03,120
And yeah, give me the chance to think twice and like kind of like allow my long-term thinking brain to kick in and override my dopamine seeking short-term brain that just wants fun, right?

42
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:06,320
And this actually, yeah, this allowed me to do that, right?

43
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,600
It just pauses me and allows me to make an intentional decision.

44
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,640
And sometimes it's okay.

45
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:15,120
Sometimes you're like, yeah, I'm bored.

46
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:18,080
I just want to look what's new on Instagram.

47
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:20,800
But then at least it's like an intentional decision.

48
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:29,200
And it's not like you accidentally sit on your couch and scroll for half an hour and then you don't even remember why you opened it and what you did on the app.

49
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,960
And that's one sec.

50
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:32,720
Very short.

51
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,960
It's not really an elevator pitch anymore.

52
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,800
But that's a long elevator.

53
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,160
It's a slow elevator.

54
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:43,520
And we'll dig into that sort of origin story here in a minute.

55
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:47,840
But first, I want to kick us off with kind of the introduction to who you are.

56
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:53,120
So the three questions I always ask to kind of kick off this conversation is where are you from?

57
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:55,840
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

58
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:01,920
And then what led you up to WONSEC more deeply than just what we just talked about?

59
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:06,160
So I'm from Stuttgart in Germany, in the south of Germany.

60
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:12,960
Two of the big German car companies come from that are struggling right now, I guess, Mercedes and Porsche.

61
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:16,800
And to be honest, I'm also, like, I'm not really a big fan of them.

62
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:22,000
Like, I guess they build really great hardware, but they struggle so much to build great software.

63
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,560
Like, they're not really fans of CarPlay, but, like, actually, when.

64
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,440
I'm using a car, all I want is CarPlay.

65
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:31,600
I don't want any of their own navigation software or like horrible.

66
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:37,120
I'm definitely in that category that treats cars as gadgets that get me from place to place.

67
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:45,600
And so, yeah, I'm definitely a sucker for like CarPlay, good sound system, smooth and quiet is really the most important thing.

68
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:51,920
They did a lot of weird things in the last couple of years where I'm like, yeah, like, what is actually your goal?

69
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:57,280
Like, are you really trying to build a great product that is beneficial for myself?

70
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:05,760
Or are you like, why are you trying to push your own software solutions so much, like the best user experience you can provide is like if it's kind of like invisible, right?

71
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:15,440
You go into the car and CarPlay already has access to my calendar and can show me like the navigation address of my next event.

72
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:20,800
It's just the tap of a button and then my podcasts are there and like it's perfect, like wow.

73
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,640
Anyway, we are getting distracted.

74
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:24,560
So that's where I'm from.

75
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:28,080
But I never had anything to do with them for like for exactly that reason.

76
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:34,160
I felt like a lost cause because they really think in a very narrow way, right?

77
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:36,800
They kind of like, yeah, we've always, always done that this way.

78
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:37,040
way.

79
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:38,160
We will never change that.

80
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:55,760
And I can just hope that kind of like this crisis going on right now will wake them up and like allow them to finally innovate a little bit again because what they have been doing in the past years is really not very attractive for me as a potential customer.

81
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:57,280
Exactly.

82
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:58,960
What was your second question again?

83
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:01,360
So yeah, so then what's your education?

84
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:05,360
Do you have a formal education related to software engineering or what's your background there?

85
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:06,240
Yes and no.

86
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:11,520
I would say not necessarily because I have started making apps when I was still in high school.

87
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:24,800
So I started kind of without formal education, but then later I actually studied software engineering at the University of Stuttgart, which was also, I think, very good for me to get more professional at making software.

88
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:32,960
Because before, I was kind of like a cowboy coder putting everything in one file, not really knowing anything about object oriented programming.

89
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,000
I just wanted the app to work.

90
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:46,800
And the university career really helped me to professionalize that and become better at like working together in a team using Git and making nice reusable code.

91
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:48,560
And you don't really need that anymore today.

92
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:50,640
that more today with all the LLMs.

93
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:58,080
But at least for the past ten years, it was very helpful to have these skills to get where you are.

94
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:01,440
So you started building apps when you were sixteen.

95
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:03,600
What was the, like, why did you start building apps?

96
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:05,120
What were the first apps that you were building?

97
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:13,520
I think it was even earlier, maybe it was, it was like a time period where I wanted to build apps, but it was so hard back then.

98
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:18,720
It was kind of like when Xcode and Interface Builder were still two separate apps.

99
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:24,560
I think that must have been like with iOS 3 or iPhoneOS 3 or iPhoneOS 4.

100
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:26,080
in that time.

101
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:32,000
And my problem was that the tutorials, they were all outdated, right?

102
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:40,800
Whenever I tried to download the new Xcode, which happened, I don't know, twice or three times a year, I was like, okay, I'm gonna try it again.

103
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:46,800
And then I would have the latest Xcode and then look at some YouTube video or some book or whatever.

104
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:50,480
And they were all based on a previous version of Xcode.

105
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:56,480
And it was like so confusing because everything looked different and the buttons were in different positions.

106
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:00,160
And in the end it was all like split across different apps.

107
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:08,960
And I struggled a lot to get started and to have like my first moment where I was like, okay, I kind of understand what I'm doing now.

108
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:14,880
And on top of that, it was also kind of expensive for me as a fourteen year old boy.

109
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:20,640
You had to pay the 99 dollar enrollment fee to even be able to get started.

110
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:28,160
I was delivering newspapers to be able to buy a white plastic MacBook back then, like for, I think it was like 900 euros.

111
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,480
And I even upgraded the RAM to two gigabytes, like a completely different world.

112
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:47,920
I think when I was like able to build like an user interface and the interface builder and have like an IB outlet or an IB action that would trigger an alert view pop up.

113
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:58,880
So when I press the button, suddenly an alert pops up and I was like, oh, now I kind of like have the connection between like a user action and then actually running code and something else happens.

114
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:03,280
What I did then was hiding and unhiding views like when you click a button.

115
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:04,640
All view, yeah, yeah.

116
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:08,400
So that's how I got started.

117
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:10,000
But why did I get started?

118
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:11,840
That's a completely different story.

119
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:20,160
I was back then, we got these Texas Instruments graphics calculators from our school for math classes.

120
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:26,160
And they were really amazing, like calculators where you could also program on.

121
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:30,960
And I was just putting in like formulars to make like homework easier to solve.

122
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,000
And that's my first programming as well, actually.

123
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:35,200
Yeah, exactly.

124
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:46,320
And then I was like, okay, I kind of like enjoy doing this and this is kind of like how I think or I guess I was also attracted to building tools that make my life easier.

125
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:51,760
But then at the same time I had like an iPod touch., which was so much more powerful than my graphics calculator.

126
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:54,560
It was actually kind of slow, these Texas instruments.

127
00:10:55,440 --> 00:11:01,600
It has a color screen, a touchscreen, some like sensors like accelerator and so on, internet connection, Wi-Fi.

128
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:06,640
And I was like, yeah, this would be much cooler to actually write software for this.

129
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,760
Plus, a lot of my friends have an iPod touch as well.

130
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:13,600
So I could build tools that we use together.

131
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:22,560
I was just trying for two years, I think, overall to have my first success experience on the iPod touch, making an app for it.

132
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:44,000
And then I did this whole cowboy coding thing where I was just hiding unhiding views and I built an app called iRedstone, which is like a Minecraft guide tutorial app that it just has a bunch of screenshots and videos and texts describing how you can build cool machines in Minecraft with the Redstone system.

133
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,160
version of a website basically.

134
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:47,680
Yeah, exactly.

135
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:55,520
I guess you could have done the same thing on a website, but of course through the App Store at Watch it was much easier to monetize.

136
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:57,040
It was a 99 cent app.

137
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:04,000
Plus, I guess also for Discovery it was much easier because back then there was no Minecraft Pocket Edition.

138
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:07,600
So when you searched on the App Store for Minecraft, my app would show up.

139
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:11,600
I guess that was a convenient coincidence that I didn't plan that.

140
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:23,840
But I guess a lot of people were like, yeah, okay, at least I can get inspired when I'm on the school bus and look at some cool mechanisms that I can later build on my computer when I'm home.

141
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,520
Plus you can put it besides your computer, I think as a website.

142
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:32,160
Back then, kind of like on an iPod touch, the web was still a little bit slow.

143
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:38,880
And then usually these websites would have a lot of ads and it's just not not like a great experience back then.

144
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:44,160
But yeah, with a native app, people were actually really enjoying to use it.

145
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:45,760
It gained a lot of attention.

146
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:56,240
I guess people were really happy to learn about this redstone system and build cool machines in Minecraft that their friends then didn't know about how to copy them.

147
00:12:56,640 --> 00:13:05,360
So you're a student, you're making a bunch of little toy apps for fun, you make this one and it kind of catches on and you're actually making money from it.

148
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:09,840
Did that change your mind in terms of where you're like, oh, this could be like a career?

149
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,840
Like, how did you go from there?

150
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:18,720
I think that was not the moment yet where I thought this could be a career because it felt very instable.

151
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:23,840
Like this, you never know how much money you make the next month and it was fluctuating a lot.

152
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:26,400
But in general it would go up.

153
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:33,040
Like 2013, I think the app became one of the top five most purchased apps in Germany.

154
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:36,320
Then I was like, okay, this is insane.

155
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:39,840
Like you can make a lot of money on the App Store apparently.

156
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:48,000
But still, I didn't think this could be a job for me because I was like 16, 17 years old, just about to make my high school diploma.

157
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:56,080
And I thought I wanted to become an electrical engineer because that's also related to that, but I'm really glad I did not.

158
00:13:56,560 --> 00:13:59,680
And someday I received an email from Apple.

159
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:05,520
I guess they were like, oh, this is kind of like suspicious that this app is making so much revenue.

160
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:12,240
And then they figured out, oh, there's actually like a person that's underage making this app.

161
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:15,600
This is not compliant with the developer guidelines.

162
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:16,080
Oh, no.

163
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:21,840
They were like nicely asking me, hey, like, this is not really okay what you're doing.

164
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:30,160
They eventually suggested that we transfer my account to my parents and they were like all at the same time, yeah, but actually we like what you're doing.

165
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:35,840
Don't you want to come to WDC in San Francisco on a student scholarship?

166
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:40,080
And I was like, okay, this is crazy.

167
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:45,120
I would have never thought that I would sit in a crowd and watch an Apple keynote at some point.

168
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:56,000
And that was actually the moment when I realized when I was in San Francisco at WDC that this is actually like making apps is like a proper job.

169
00:14:57,280 --> 00:15:02,160
Before it was all just like, yeah, like I do that in my bedroom after school.

170
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:11,600
Yeah, it makes some money, but like, yeah, I should get like a proper job to be sure that in ten years I still make money from it.

171
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:12,320
Okay.

172
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:16,480
So, and then, like you said, you eventually got a software engineering degree.

173
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:21,920
Were you building and releasing apps throughout this whole process?

174
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:24,320
I never stopped making apps, basically.

175
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:28,880
I had times where I was making one app per week, and that's correct.

176
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:30,560
And OneTech was actually one of them.

177
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:37,840
You know, most people have an idea, they'll sit there and chew on it and work on it, and it's not ready., it's not ready.

178
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:43,440
But like, how do you get into a mode where you're releasing a new app every single week?

179
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:46,480
Like, that's a pretty intensely tight deadline.

180
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:56,000
Especially back then when the tooling was so much more complicated and actually I wasn't even capable of using the tools in the right way.

181
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:02,000
I could have saved, I could have probably made one app per day if I knew how to use a new iView controller back then.

182
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,080
So yeah, why one a week?

183
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:09,040
Was it just like each one was an idea?

184
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,160
You wanted to try it, you put it out there, and then you just move on to the next thing?

185
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:16,560
I just had time, I was bored, like that was my hobby basically.

186
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:20,480
When I came back from school, I was like, yeah, I'm just going to make a new app.

187
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:26,080
And usually when I have an idea, I cannot stop thinking about it until I actually do it.

188
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:42,560
Usually it was like, I have an idea, I build it, and then put it on the App Store, and then I have the next idea, and then maybe like the app from three weeks ago gets some attention, and then I go back to that, and I'm like, okay, I'm going to build an update for it, for the highly most requested features.

189
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:50,880
And the apps that never got traction, they were just sitting there until either Apple or myself removed them from sale at some point.

190
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:55,520
Do you know how many apps you ultimately have released in the App Store?

191
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:05,680
It must be more than fifty because when Helm came out, I broke their first prototype because they did not yet implement the paging API.

192
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:12,960
Yes, Helm, we've had them on the show, but that's an app that's like it's a Mac app that lets you use App Store Connect.

193
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:20,720
You logged in with your developer account and their early version didn't have the ability to page because they weren't expecting that many apps.

194
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,400
Yeah, exactly.

195
00:17:22,720 --> 00:17:26,000
I opened it and I was like searching for one sec and it didn't show up.

196
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:29,360
And then I sent a message, hey, like your app is broken.

197
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:30,560
I can't find one sec.

198
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:33,360
Like this is the reason why I want to use your app.

199
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:45,200
And then they discovered that eventually, oh yeah, you're kind of like, you have so many apps on your account that we have to implement like the paging API to go to page two or three, whatever.

200
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:53,040
So I think, like, I don't have a count on it, but it's somewhere between, I guess, fifty to one hundred, more towards one hundred, maybe eighty, ninety.

201
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:54,000
Goodness.

202
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:54,560
Wow.

203
00:17:54,960 --> 00:17:55,360
So okay.

204
00:17:55,840 --> 00:17:56,960
But you're not doing that now.

