00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:41,040
It was just another one of your apps.

227
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:41,680
No, not at all.

228
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,040
It was kind of like how I made every other app before.

229
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:56,720
It was like a personal interest or a personal problem I had and I was like, oh, this could be a nice way to make make a new app or explore like a new technology.

230
00:19:57,280 --> 00:20:03,280
And yeah, OneZeq was just one of those apps where I never planned to scale it or make something big out of it.

231
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:08,080
And when I released it, it just felt like, oh, next week I'm going to work on something else.

232
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:11,280
But yeah, it blew up when I launched it.

233
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:22,400
I put a screen recording on Twitter and it got a couple of thousand of likes and retreats on the first couple of days and the app was had like suddenly a couple of thousand users.

234
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:32,320
Did it break out of your circle or was it just within your sort of circle on social media it really resonated with people and then it kind of worked its way out?

235
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:36,160
I didn't have a lot of followers back then, maybe 700 or so on Twitter.

236
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:49,040
And I guess it resonated with a lot of people because everyone was like back then in the COVID times like scrolling a lot and like people were sick of that and everyone was like yeah.

237
00:20:49,920 --> 00:20:54,320
I actually want to do something useful with my time, but I always end up on Twitter or whatever.

238
00:20:54,560 --> 00:21:02,240
And then you have kind of like a super simple idea of like a kind of like a clever way to use Apple's APIs.

239
00:21:02,960 --> 00:21:10,160
And I guess in my developer friend circle that resonated a lot from kind of like this technical perspective.

240
00:21:10,400 --> 00:21:17,120
But then, of course, there was like a whole ADHD bubble that got into this and it kind of like evolved from there.

241
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:22,480
So it was pretty immediate then that you felt this is different than the other apps that you've been doing.

242
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:23,360
Yeah.

243
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:25,280
It hit me really hard.

244
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:31,360
I was like, I couldn't sleep because there was so much attention on the app suddenly.

245
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:41,840
So many users trying it and being blown away how well it works for them and how quickly they could gain back control for their social media habits.

246
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:54,640
And I was just overwhelmed by seeing the impact of the app and kind of like having found something that resonates with more than just like a super small niche of people.

247
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:58,560
Because you were just releasing things right away.

248
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:00,160
It wasn't like this was in test flight.

249
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:01,600
It was out there for real people.

250
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:15,200
So when you had this big influx of users, did it immediately start sucking your time because of like people have feature requests or bug reports and like that's where kind of all of your energy started naturally going?

251
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:17,200
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

252
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:26,720
I built the first prototype on a weekend, put it on my own phone, I used it myself two weeks, and my screen time was reduced by 50% after that.

253
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:28,640
And I was like, okay, this is really effective.

254
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:37,520
Actually, when I installed the app on my own phone, 20 seconds afterwards, I accidentally ended up opening Twitter and I ran into my own app.

255
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:41,040
And I was like, really, okay, I'm onto something here.

256
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:47,040
This even I as a developer am kind of like fooled by it or benefit from having it right away.

257
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:54,640
This just shows how addicted we are all to social media and how well a solution like OneSec can help to combat that.

258
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:58,160
And yeah, we're using it for two weeks and then I was like, okay, this works great.

259
00:22:58,320 --> 00:23:01,280
I'm going to put it on the App Store and I'm going to see what happens.

260
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:11,440
And then yeah, when I saw the resonance, I really spent two weeks to build a proper, good version of it and make it a lot nicer, a lot more polished.

261
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:19,360
And then after two weeks, like the first big update came and basically since then I have never stopped working on it.

262
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:27,040
But it was still a long time until I did it full time and stopped more or less working on my other apps.

263
00:23:28,080 --> 00:23:29,520
So how did you get to that point then?

264
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:37,840
Because initially it sounds like it was pretty successful, but it wasn't like this is sustaining you full time and definitely not you're hiring employees right away.

265
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:40,480
Because at this point, it's everything was fully indie, right?

266
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:42,640
It's not like you had any employees or anything.

267
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,520
Until today, actually, fully indie.

268
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:47,920
Well, but you have employees now, right?

269
00:23:48,240 --> 00:23:48,720
Yes, yes.

270
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:52,000
Indie in the sense that you haven't taken on funding or anything like that.

271
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:53,760
Yes, no easy money, yeah.

272
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:54,480
Right, right.

273
00:23:55,040 --> 00:24:00,000
So from the point of solo to starting to bring on help.

274
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:02,880
What was the trajectory of its growth?

