00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:19,440
But actually the people that purchased it back then, for what was it, like 399 or something, they still have access to all the pro features until today.

309
00:26:20,240 --> 00:26:24,480
I'm super thankful for their early support, so I'm very happy if they are still using OneSec, which..

310
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:30,240
a lot of them actually do, they can still use all the pro features that we have added over the years.

311
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:44,560
So once you switched over to subscription, once you realize like this is going to be taking up your sort of time and you're going to keep investing in it, you're building a bigger and bigger customer base that can fund you.

312
00:26:45,360 --> 00:26:53,760
When was the point where you realized like I either need to or can bring somebody on to start helping accelerate things more?

313
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:58,000
I think that was two years after I launched one sec.

314
00:26:58,560 --> 00:26:58,640
Okay.

315
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:04,240
I was spending like my whole day just replying to emails.

316
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:06,960
And which is like a really nice question.

317
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:09,040
That's often the first hire people make.

318
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:09,840
Yeah.

319
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:20,240
It was like a really nice task for one sec because most people start their email with something like, Oh, your app has changed my life and I'm so thankful for it.

320
00:27:21,120 --> 00:27:41,440
It's very positive, but then of course there's always this like, But I'm having this bug and kind of like, Yeah, it was just too much time and too much energy to put in that and I felt like I want to focus on building the product and I don't want to deal with all this customer support emails anymore or I just can't do it anymore.

321
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:42,480
It was too much.

322
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:52,800
And at the same time I was making like 10k MRR and I felt like okay, maybe now is the right time to start thinking about hiring my first person.

323
00:27:53,440 --> 00:27:55,120
Did it feel like scary?

324
00:27:56,400 --> 00:28:01,920
It was scary in the sense of like who am I to tell someone else what they should do with their life?

325
00:28:02,560 --> 00:28:12,640
You want to contribute something to one sec, but then I have to give you feedback and tell you my honest opinion and what if I don't like it, I have to tell you that.

326
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:22,400
And I was really anxious to do that because I felt like I'm not entitled to tell someone else what they should do with their time and with their life.

327
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:31,920
But I quickly realized that it's actually a very beneficial relationship for both of us because the person, Lucas, that I hired, he was super happy.

328
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:33,040
He was a student back then.

329
00:28:33,600 --> 00:28:37,520
He was super happy to have a job with very flexible hours.

330
00:28:37,840 --> 00:28:41,600
Like it doesn't matter if he replys to emails at 3 am or 3 pm.

331
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:43,120
He can work on the weekend.

332
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:43,600
He can.

333
00:28:44,320 --> 00:28:48,000
It's a lot cooler than like working at a factory or fast food or anything.

334
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:52,800
And on top of that, he works on a product that he actually cares about.

335
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:53,760
He uses it himself.

336
00:28:54,160 --> 00:28:57,920
Everyone in our company uses the app themselves and they're fans of the app.

337
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:00,480
Like everyone is affected by it.

338
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:12,560
And so there's like a deeper purpose in the work and it took me a while to understand that people are actually grateful to be able to contribute to something to OneSeq.

339
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:24,000
Did you already have someone in mind or were you like, I need to hire someone and then you had to go through a process of finding someone who fits your work style and has that same passion?

340
00:29:25,200 --> 00:29:30,800
The funny thing is I hired almost all my employees or freelancers through social media.

341
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:36,800
So yeah, if you want to get a job at Wondsack, follow me on Instagram.

342
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:42,960
Because sometimes I just put a story, hey, we need a new person to join our customer support team.

343
00:29:43,440 --> 00:29:44,160
Do you know anyone?

344
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:48,400
And then usually it's like a friend of a friend or directly a friend.

345
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:59,680
And in the end, that just shows that social media is a great tool and I don't want to stop using it completely because there's still a lot of value in connecting us and allowing things to happen.

346
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:05,520
I've always appreciated about OneSec and all of your sort of branding around it is that ethos.

347
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:22,480
I feel like oftentimes people in this space treat social media like it's the devil, like it's a vice, even when they have the same type of feature set where it's like screen time is like helping you reduce it, not completely get rid of it.

348
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:24,720
But it's always in this, it's like sugar.

349
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:26,000
It's like, well, you don't need any.

350
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:28,480
It's all bad, but everybody's going to give in a little.

351
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:36,800
And I appreciate that you always highlight how, like, no, these are like beneficial things to creating networks in society.

352
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:40,640
and for me like keeping up with families spread around the world, like these are good things.

353
00:30:41,200 --> 00:30:47,840
It's just they're weaponized by people who are psychologically, of course.

354
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:48,960
Yeah, exactly.

355
00:30:49,440 --> 00:30:51,520
Like you could say the same thing about the iPhone, right?

356
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:59,280
For me, it's like the best invention humanity has ever done, but of course, you could also argue it's kind of like an addiction machine that enables social media to do what they do.

357
00:30:59,600 --> 00:31:00,720
But I disagree with that.

358
00:31:00,960 --> 00:31:17,600
I think, yeah, the iPhone is a really great tool and social media is also a great tool, not as great as the iPhone, but still it enables us to, yeah, stay connected all the world and maybe we wouldn't have met if social media didn't exist.

359
00:31:17,760 --> 00:31:22,080
Almost like just enabled so many, so many things and connections.

360
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:36,160
And I think, like, if you think social media is the devil, which I can agree, like they do a lot of evil shit on their apps to keep us hooked, you can also delete it completely, right?

361
00:31:36,400 --> 00:31:39,600
You don't have to build like complete blockers or something.

362
00:31:40,400 --> 00:31:43,040
Just delete it and you're fine, right?

363
00:31:43,280 --> 00:31:45,680
Or, I don't know, get rid of your phone or whatever.

364
00:31:46,480 --> 00:32:59,920
You hired someone to help with customer support and it sounds like that went really well presumably did free up your time and the relationship worked out and then you've done a lot more since then so where did you kind of move from then yeah when I had the time again to focus on building the product I focused also a lot on marketing and I quickly realized that Instagram especially is a great channel to acquire new customers like kind of like previously how I did it on Twitter I brought that over to Instagram because of course on Instagram you have like a completely different target group of users at least in in Europe or in in Germany Twitter is or nowadays even less but back then Twitter was only really used by journalists and some researchers but like the normal people they they would use Instagram as a social media and then yeah I started making content for Instagram as well and was also paying for that content to be shown to more people and I figure out that works really well because of course On social media, I find my target group of people that spend a lot of time on social media and want to spend less.

365
00:33:00,560 --> 00:33:07,200
It's kind of an ironic, like perfect targeting, where it's like the exact group of people that you want.

366
00:33:07,760 --> 00:33:14,240
It's like advertising to sports fans on your favorite sports team's forum or something.

367
00:33:14,480 --> 00:33:17,200
It's like 100% of the people are interested in this topic here.

368
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:20,160
The difference is it's not some niche place.

369
00:33:20,400 --> 00:33:23,280
It's the biggest social network in the entire world.

370
00:33:24,240 --> 00:33:28,640
But literally every single person there is potentially in your Tam, which is kind of wild.

371
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:30,160
Yeah, exactly.

372
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:30,640
I agree.

373
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:39,920
It's kind of like back when I did Redpoint, my climbing fitness tracker for Apple Watch, I was putting posters in climbing gyms because that's where my target group is.

374
00:33:40,080 --> 00:33:44,320
And I guess subconsciously I took that learning to WONSEC as well.

375
00:33:44,880 --> 00:33:50,720
And when I started doing that, I could see that my revenue was kind of like doubling every single month.

376
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:53,840
And I was like, okay, well, like this is insane.

377
00:33:54,080 --> 00:33:55,760
And next month it was more again.

378
00:33:56,080 --> 00:34:11,840
And then suddenly a lot of famous people discovered WONSEC and they were putting WONSEC in their stories because, of course, if you're like a famous person with a lot of followers, you are even more likely to have negative effects from social media.

379
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:18,960
Like, the more likes you have, the more views you get, the more attention you get, I guess the more dependent your brain becomes on all of these.

380
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:28,640
And then you're also much more thankful or grateful to find a solution like OneSec and to realize, oh, this actually helps me and this is really effective.

381
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:32,960
And then people are just like, okay, everyone has to know about this tool, this is so amazing.

382
00:34:33,120 --> 00:34:37,040
And they just made like a video and put it on there, put it on a reel or on TikTok.

383
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:40,720
And yeah, it really blew up from there.

384
00:34:41,120 --> 00:34:42,320
So you were focusing on that marketing.

385
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:47,680
Did you ever hire people to help with the marketing or is that still kind of one of the areas where you're spending a lot of your time?

386
00:34:48,560 --> 00:34:54,800
I hired someone to help me with marketing, but I would still say that mostly I was in charge.

387
00:34:55,040 --> 00:34:57,200
Like she is supporting me with it.

388
00:34:57,360 --> 00:34:58,640
She is also creating content.

389
00:34:59,520 --> 00:35:09,600
But we work together on our concepts and on our ideas because I think in the end, marketing is equally as important as the product.

390
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:13,520
And that's also something I had to learn the hard way kind of order.

391
00:35:14,320 --> 00:35:15,520
I didn't know it from the beginning.

392
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:22,720
But yeah, I quickly realized, of course, if you build a product and nobody knows about it, it can be amazing, but it just doesn't work.

393
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:26,160
And I guess that's Apple is also a great example of that.

